# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. The parable is told at a meal. “And it came to pass, as he went into the house of one of the chief Pharisees to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him” (14:1). A man with the dropsy stands before Jesus, and Jesus asks the lawyers and Pharisees, “Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath day?” (14:3). They hold their peace, and He heals the man and lets him go (14:4). Then Luke says, “he put forth a parable to those which were bidden, when he marked how they chose out the chief rooms” (14:7).'))
A(('p', '2.2 Where this parable stands. The great supper is told after two sets of sayings at this table, following the healing. First He speaks to the guests about the highest and the lowest room (14:8–11). Then He speaks “to him that bade him” (14:12) about whom to call when he makes a dinner or a supper: “call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind” (14:13). He adds, “thou shalt be recompensed at the resurrection of the just” (14:14). Only then does the parable begin, and it begins as an answer: “Then said he unto him” (14:16).'))
A(('call', 'A Remark Introduces This Parable',
   'The parable follows a remark from one of the guests: “And when one of them that sat at meat with him heard these things, he said unto him, Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God” (14:15). Jesus’ reply begins, “A certain man made a great supper, and bade many” (14:16). Luke does not name the speaker and does not say what he meant by his words. The text records the remark and the reply.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. The chapter before has Jesus teaching as He goes through the cities and villages, “journeying toward Jerusalem” (13:22). He says, “they shall come from the east, and from the west, and from the north, and from the south, and shall sit down in the kingdom of God” (13:29), and, “there are last which shall be first, and there are first which shall be last” (13:30). Luke 14 does not quote those words and does not refer back to them. They are part of the setting that stands before the meal, and they are not used here to define any part of the parable.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Luke has already shown what Jesus preached and what He sent others to preach. He says, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). He “sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (9:2), and He told the seventy to say, “The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (10:9). The remark at the table, “Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God,” uses the same phrase, the kingdom of God, that Jesus had been preaching to the people of that land.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives no statement of purpose for the parable. He gives the sequence: the sayings to the guests and to the host, the remark about eating bread in the kingdom of God, and then the story of a supper. This study states no reason beyond that sequence.'))
