# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The great supper and the first call (14:16–17). “A certain man made a great supper, and bade many: And sent his servant at supper time to say to them that were bidden, Come; for all things are now ready.” The sequence has four steps. A certain man makes a great supper. He bids many. At supper time he sends his servant. The servant says, “Come,” and gives the reason, “for all things are now ready.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 The excuses (14:18–20). “And they all with one consent began to make excuse. The first said unto him, I have bought a piece of ground, and I must needs go and see it: I pray thee have me excused. And another said, I have bought five yoke of oxen, and I go to prove them: I pray thee have me excused. And another said, I have married a wife, and therefore I cannot come.” Luke says they began “all with one consent.” Three speakers are given. The first has bought a piece of ground and says he must needs go and see it. The second has bought five yoke of oxen and goes to prove them. The third has married a wife and says he cannot come. The first two use the words “I pray thee have me excused.” The third does not.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The report and the master’s anger (14:21). “So that servant came, and shewed his lord these things. Then the master of the house being angry said to his servant, Go out quickly into the streets and lanes of the city, and bring in hither the poor, and the maimed, and the halt, and the blind.” The servant returns and shows his lord “these things.” The text calls the same person “his lord” and “the master of the house.” He is angry. He sends the servant out “quickly” into the streets and lanes of the city, to bring in four kinds of people: the poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The second sending (14:22–23). “And the servant said, Lord, it is done as thou hast commanded, and yet there is room. And the lord said unto the servant, Go out into the highways and hedges, and compel them to come in, that my house may be filled.” The servant says the command is done, and that there is still room. The lord sends him out again, now into the highways and hedges, to compel them to come in. The lord gives a purpose: “that my house may be filled.”'))
A(('p', '3.5 The closing statement (14:24). “For I say unto you, That none of those men which were bidden shall taste of my supper.” The lord has spoken to the servant in the singular, “thou.” This verse says “you.” The text does not say whom “you” names, and it does not say whether these are the lord’s words inside the story or Jesus’ own words to the table. The statement is recorded as written. It says that none of those men which were bidden shall taste of “my supper.”'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 14:16', '“made a great supper, and bade many”', 'One man, one supper, many bidden.'],
    ['Luke 14:17', '“Come; for all things are now ready”', 'The call, and the reason given for it.'],
    ['Luke 14:18–20', '“all with one consent began to make excuse”', 'Three excuses, each with its own wording.'],
    ['Luke 14:21', '“the master of the house being angry”', 'The report is followed by anger and a sending.'],
    ['Luke 14:22', '“yet there is room”', 'The servant reports the command done.'],
    ['Luke 14:23', '“compel them to come in, that my house may be filled”', 'The second sending, with its stated purpose.'],
    ['Luke 14:24', '“none of those men which were bidden shall taste of my supper”', 'The closing statement.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.6 What the story contains. Twelve things can be counted in the text: a certain man, a great supper, many bidden, a servant, supper time, the word “Come,” three excuses, a report, the master’s anger, the streets and lanes of the city, the highways and hedges, and the closing statement. The man makes, bids and sends. The servant calls, reports and goes out. The bidden give only reasons for not coming.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the certain man is. Jesus does not say what the supper is. Jesus does not say who the many bidden are or why the first call went to them. Jesus does not say who the servant is. Jesus does not say what the ground, the oxen or the wife stand for. Jesus does not say who the poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind are. Jesus does not say what the streets and lanes of the city are, or what the highways and hedges are. Jesus does not say what it is to “compel them to come in,” or who comes in. The text does not say that the house was filled. It gives the purpose, “that my house may be filled,” and then the closing statement. The text does not say whom “you” names in 14:24. The parable has no wedding, no son, no king, no garment and no violence.'))
A(('p', 'The four kinds of people in 14:21 are named in words close to those of 14:13: “the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind” there, and “the poor, and the maimed, and the halt, and the blind” here. Jesus does not say that the two lists name the same people, and this study does not say it either.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. Luke records an explanation of the Sower, where the disciples asked, “What might this parable be?” and Jesus answered, “Now the parable is this: The seed is the word of God” (8:9, 11). For the great supper he records none. No one asks what it means, and Jesus does not say. After 14:24 the scene turns: “And there went great multitudes with him: and he turned, and said unto them” (14:25), and what follows is a new saying on a new subject. This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not identify. The parable names persons, places and things and leaves each of them without an explanation. Jesus does not identify the certain man. Jesus does not identify the supper. Jesus does not identify the many who were bidden. Jesus does not identify the servant. Jesus does not identify the ground, the oxen or the wife. Jesus does not identify the poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind. Jesus does not identify the streets and lanes of the city. Jesus does not identify the highways and hedges. Jesus does not identify those who are compelled to come in. Jesus does not identify the house. Jesus does not identify the “you” of 14:24.'))
