A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the man is a certain person, that the bidden are a certain people, that the poor and the blind are a certain people, or that the highways are a certain mission is supplying what the Lord did not. The explanation of the Sower in Luke 8:11 belongs to that parable, and nothing in it is carried into this one.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What the text does say. The text says that a certain man made a great supper and bade many; that at supper time his servant told the bidden to come, for all things were ready; that all with one consent made excuse; that the master of the house, being angry, sent his servant into the streets and lanes for the poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind; that there was yet room; that the lord sent him to the highways and hedges to compel them to come in, that the house might be filled; and that none of those men which were bidden shall taste of his supper. These statements are made by the story itself and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for this parable, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus does not open this story by saying that the kingdom of God is like it. He answers a remark about eating bread in the kingdom of God with a story about a supper that is made, a call that goes out, excuses that are given, and a house that the lord wants filled. The meaning is stated at the level of that story and its answer to the remark.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    'The supper is ready. The servant’s word is “Come; for all things are now ready,” and the call is for the present moment, at supper time.',
    'Those bidden can decline, and the story shows them doing it, with reasons that come from ordinary things: a piece of ground, five yoke of oxen, a marriage.',
    'Refusal has a result. The master is angry, and the closing statement says that none of those men which were bidden shall taste of the supper.',
    'The invitation goes outward. It goes to the streets and lanes of the city, and then to the highways and hedges.',
    'The lord’s purpose holds. He says, “that my house may be filled.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were lawyers and Pharisees, those bidden to the meal, and the host (14:3, 7, 12). They had just heard Jesus speak of the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind (14:13), and of a recompence “at the resurrection of the just” (14:14). One of them had said that he is blessed who shall eat bread in the kingdom of God (14:15). They then heard a story in which a supper is ready, the bidden do not come, and others are brought in. Jesus does not say what they were to conclude, and Luke does not say what they concluded.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the contrast between the call and the reply. The servant says that all things are now ready, and the answers are “I must needs go,” “I go to prove them” and “I cannot come.” It is in the contrast between the many who were bidden and the ones brought in, and between the master’s first purpose for the supper and the way the house is filled. It is in the closing statement, which fixes the outcome for those who were bidden.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. A ready summons calls for an answer now. Ordinary concerns can be given as reasons for refusing it. The refusal has consequences. The invitation expands outward. The host’s house is to be filled. Speaking of a feast is not the same as coming to it.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the host, the servant, the bidden or those brought in are. It does not say what the supper, the streets, the highways or the house are. It does not say what “compel” involves, or turn the three excuses into a list of sins. It does not carry anything in from another parable or from the sayings at the same table.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The great banquet is kept apart here from the stories and sayings that stand near it or sound like it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 22:1–14', '“a certain king, which made a marriage for his son”; “a man which had not on a wedding garment”; “For many are called, but few are chosen”', 'The Wedding Banquet. A king, a son, a wedding, servants who are slain, a city burned, a garment inspected. None of these is in Luke 14.'],
    ['Luke 14:7–14', '“sit not down in the highest room”; “call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind”', 'The sayings at the same table, spoken before the parable. They are not part of the story and are not its explanation.'],
    ['Luke 12:16–21', '“The ground of a certain rich man brought forth plentifully”; “Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee”', 'The Rich Fool. A different man, a different setting and a different ending. The word “ground” appears in both, and the stories are not the same.'],
    ['Luke 14:28–32', '“intending to build a tower”; “what king, going to make war against another king”', 'The Tower and the Warring King. They stand just after the great supper in the same chapter, and are told to “great multitudes” (14:25).'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The great banquet is told at a Sabbath meal in a chief Pharisee’s house, in answer to a remark from a guest. In Matthew 22:1 Jesus “answered and spake unto them again by parables”; the verse gives no meal and no remark from a guest. The Rich Fool is told after one of the company asked Jesus to speak to his brother about an inheritance (Luke 12:13). The sayings of 14:8–14 are spoken at the same table but are not part of the story.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. In Luke the host is “a certain man,” “his lord” and “the master of the house.” There is one servant. In Matthew the host is “a certain king,” the feast is “a marriage for his son,” and the servants are many. In Luke there are three speakers with three excuses. In Matthew some make light of it and go their ways, “one to his farm, another to his merchandise,” and “the remnant” take the servants and slay them (22:5–6). Luke has no son, no king, no garment and no guest who is questioned.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. Luke moves from the bidding and the call, to the excuses, to the master’s anger, to a sending to the streets and lanes, to a report that there is yet room, to a sending to the highways and hedges, and to a closing statement. Matthew moves from a first sending, to a second, to the slaying of servants, to the king’s armies and the burning of the city, to a sending to the highways, to a hall furnished with guests, to the king’s question to one man, and to “many are called, but few are chosen.” The movements are not the same, and one is not a summary of the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the excuses of Luke 14:18–20 are not the violence of Matthew 22:6, and neither is read as the other. Second, there is no garment in Luke 14, and the question put to a guest in Matthew 22:12 is not part of this parable. Third, “compel them to come in” is a sentence in Luke 14:23 and has no counterpart in Matthew 22:9. Fourth, the “ground” of Luke 14:18 and the “ground” of Luke 12:16 are the same word in two different stories, and each belongs to its own story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the kingdom of God (4:43), sent the Twelve to preach it (9:2), and told the seventy to say, “The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (10:9). The great banquet belongs to that ministry. It is told at a meal in a chief Pharisee’s house to lawyers and Pharisees, to those who were bidden, and to the host (14:3, 7, 12), and it answers a remark about eating bread in the kingdom of God.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The supper in the parable and “the kingdom of God” of 14:15 belong to the Kingdom Jesus preached in His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The host, the servant, the bidden, the supper and the house are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation or its destiny. The great supper of this parable is not the Body’s destiny. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The words “come,” “excuse,” “bring in” and “compel” are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of readiness.** The call is “Come; for all things are now ready.” A reader learns that when a plain call is ready, it asks for an answer and not for delay.',
    '**A lesson of priorities.** The excuses come from a piece of ground, five yoke of oxen and a marriage. A reader learns that ordinary things, which the text does not call wrong, can crowd out a call that is plainly given.',
    '**A lesson of open hospitality.** The story is told after Jesus tells the host to call the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind (14:13). A reader learns to keep a table open to those who cannot repay.',
    '**A lesson against empty words.** One guest spoke of the blessing of eating bread in the kingdom of God. A reader learns that speaking well of a feast is not the same as coming to it. The text does not say the speaker spoke in presumption.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom supper is not turned into the Body’s destiny, and Israel’s warnings are not turned into the Body’s identity.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus answers a guest’s remark about eating bread in the kingdom of God with a story. A certain man makes a great supper and bids many. At supper time his servant says, “Come; for all things are now ready.” All with one consent make excuse: a piece of ground, five yoke of oxen, a marriage. The master of the house, angry, sends the servant to the streets and lanes for the poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind. There is yet room, and he sends the servant to the highways and hedges to compel them to come in, that his house may be filled. He says that none of those men which were bidden shall taste of his supper. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. Any reading that adds a name, an identity or a doctrine goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a great supper; many bidden; the call, “Come”; three excuses; the master’s anger; the streets and lanes; “yet there is room”; the highways and hedges; the closing statement.',
    '**The meaning:** the supper is ready; the bidden can refuse and the refusal has a result; the invitation goes outward; the lord’s house is to be filled.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the man, the servant, the bidden, the poor, the streets, the highways or the house; nothing carried in from the Wedding Banquet.',
    '**The present lesson:** readiness, priorities, open hospitality and the difference between speaking of a feast and coming to it, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

