A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between characters.** In Luke the host is a certain man and his lord, with one servant. He is not a king, and there is no son. The bidden give three excuses. The poor, the maimed, the halt and the blind are a second group, and those in the highways and hedges are named only as “them.”',
    '**Between actions.** The man makes the supper and bids. The servant calls, reports and goes out. The bidden make excuse. The master is angry and sends twice. The lord states his purpose and then the closing statement.',
    '**Between settings.** The parable stands in a Sabbath meal at a chief Pharisee’s house, after the sayings of 14:8–14 and before the great multitudes of 14:25.',
    '**Between this parable and its neighbors.** The king, the son, the slain servants, the burned city and the garment belong to the Wedding Banquet. The rich man’s barns belong to the Rich Fool. The tower and the king’s war belong to the sayings of 14:28–32.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the man, the servant, the bidden, the poor, the maimed, the halt, the blind, the streets, the highways or the house. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from Matthew 22, from Luke 12:16–21, or from 14:7–14.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Coming, refusing, bringing in and compelling are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

